    57	## Section guidance
    58	
    59	### Hero
    60	- **Headline direction:** Name the procedure and location clearly — "Gastric sleeve surgery in Perth". No cleverness needed; this is an SEO anchor.
    61	- **Subheadline:** 2–3 sentences that validate the reader's experience (years of trying, weight returning, feelings of failure) and reframe the situation as a medical one, not a willpower one. Establish that this team will help the reader decide whether the sleeve is the right fit — not sell them on it.
    62	
    63	---
    64	
    65	### Stats bar (4 stats)
    66	Use these verbatim. They are factual anchors and must not be paraphrased or inflated.
    67	
    68	| Label | Value | Note |
    69	|---|---|---|
    70	| Excess weight loss | 60–70% | Average over the first 12–18 months. Individual results vary. |
    71	| Procedure time | 45–60 min | Performed laparoscopically (keyhole). |
    72	| Hospital stay | 2 nights | At SJOG Murdoch or Subiaco. |
    73	| Back to desk work | ~2 weeks | 4–6 weeks for physical labour. |
    74	
    75	---
    76	
    77	### What is a gastric sleeve?
    78	- ~3 paragraphs
    79	- **Para 1:** Define the procedure. Explain that roughly 75–80% of the stomach is removed, leaving a narrow banana-shaped tube that holds far less food. Use plain language — no jargon.
    80	- **Para 2:** Explain the hormonal mechanism — the removed portion produces most of the body's ghrelin (hunger hormone), so some patients may experience reduced appetite after surgery, not just smaller capacity. This is the "why it works differently from a diet" paragraph.
    81	- **Para 3:** Be direct about permanence — this is an irreversible change to anatomy. Then distinguish from a bypass: the sleeve does not reroute the intestines, so food still travels through the digestive system in its normal order. Frame this as context for decision-making, not a selling point.
    82	
    83	**Comparison note to weave in:** A short paragraph (or callout) comparing sleeve vs bypass — a bypass reroutes the small intestine to reduce calorie absorption; a sleeve does not reroute anything. Neither is universally better. The right choice depends on the patient's weight, health conditions, reflux history and surgeon discussion.
    84	
    85	---
    86	
    87	### Eligibility
    88	- **MBS/BMI note:** For Medicare item 31575, eligibility generally requires a BMI of 40 or higher, or a BMI of 35 or higher with a major comorbidity. BMI is only a starting point; suitability and eligibility are assessed individually by the surgeon.
    89	- **Bullet points to address:**
    90	  1. BMI is a starting point only — the surgeon assesses the whole health picture, not just a number.
    91	  2. The reader has tried to lose weight through diet and lifestyle changes without lasting success. Normalise this — it is expected, not a mark against them.
    92	  3. The reader must be prepared to commit to long-term dietary changes and ongoing follow-up. Surgery is a tool, not a one-off fix.
    93	  4. Some health conditions may need management before surgery; suitability is assessed individually and no outcome or safety is guaranteed.
    94	  5. Close with a gentle nudge: the only real way to know whether they qualify is an assessment. An eligibility check is the simple place to begin.
    95	
    96	---
    97	
    98	### How it works (6 steps)
    99	Each step gets a short heading and 1–2 sentences of content direction.
   100	
   101	1. **Initial consultation and assessment** — The reader meets their surgeon, talks through medical history and goals, and gets an honest view of whether the sleeve suits them. This is also where they can ask the questions that feel awkward to raise.
   102	2. **Pre-surgery preparation** — The patient may need to follow a specific pre-op diet (typically 2–4 weeks) to shrink the liver and reduce surgical risk. Emphasise that the team supports them through this — not just a handout.
   103	3. **The procedure** — General anaesthetic, keyhole technique, 45–60 minutes. Surgeon removes ~75–80% of the stomach and seals the new sleeve with a staple line. Keep it clinical but accessible.
   104	4. **Hospital recovery** — Around 2 nights at St John of God Murdoch or Subiaco. Patient starts on fluids and progresses under guidance from nursing and dietetic staff.
   105	5. **Returning to daily life** — Desk work after ~2 weeks; physical or manual labour closer to 4–6 weeks. Surgeon provides advice tailored to the individual's situation.
   106	6. **Long-term follow-up** — Staged eating progression (fluids → soft → regular textures) with dietitian support. Ongoing reviews and blood tests to protect nutrition and results long-term.
   107	
   108	---
   109	
   110	### Benefits (6 bullets)
   111	Each bullet should convey one clear, qualified benefit. Do not use superlatives.
   112	
   113	1. Weight loss — clinical averages of 60–70% of excess weight over 12–18 months are reported in some studies; individual results vary and no result is guaranteed.
   114	2. Reduced appetite — the procedure lowers ghrelin production, and some people may find smaller portions more satisfying; individual experiences vary.
   115	3. Improvement in weight-related conditions — type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, sleep apnoea, joint pain. Qualify that outcomes differ between individuals.
   116	4. No intestinal rerouting — food travels through the digestive tract in its normal order; the sleeve is generally less malabsorptive than a bypass, although vitamin and mineral monitoring is still needed.
   117	5. Keyhole approach — smaller incisions than open surgery; pain, hospital stay and recovery vary by individual and surgical circumstances.
   118	6. Single permanent procedure — no implanted device to adjust or maintain, unlike a gastric band.
   119	
   120	---
   121	
   122	### Risks / honest section
   123	- **Intro paragraph tone:** Acknowledge directly that gastric sleeve surgery is major surgery with real risks. The practice would rather the reader hear these plainly now than be surprised later. The surgeon will go through each risk in the context of the individual's health before any decision is made.
   124	- **Risk bullets to address:**
   125	  1. Staple line leak — occurs in roughly 1–3% of cases; the most serious early complication, monitored closely and may require further treatment.
   126	  2. Reflux or heartburn — can be new or worsened after a sleeve; symptoms may settle for some and persist for others; it may influence the choice of procedure.
   127	  3. Nutritional deficiencies — particularly vitamin B12, iron and vitamin D; lifelong supplements and regular blood tests are part of staying well.
   128	  4. Hair thinning — can occur in the months after surgery and may be associated with rapid weight loss or reduced nutritional intake; patients should discuss persistent symptoms or nutritional concerns with their care team.
   129	  5. Weight regain — possible if eating habits drift and follow-up lapses; the sleeve works best alongside ongoing support.
   130	  6. General surgical risks — bleeding, infection, blood clots and anaesthetic reactions, as with any operation.
   131	
   132	---
   133	
   134	### Cost paragraph
   135	Cover these points in a single flowing paragraph (not a list):
   136	- Total cost varies depending on private health cover, surgeon and anaesthetist fees, hospital charges and any out-of-pocket gap.
   137	- Signal honesty about cost upfront — uncertainty about money is a major source of stress.
   138	- Medicare item number 31575 attracts a rebate when eligibility criteria are met.
   139	- Private health insurance with appropriate hospital cover may contribute to hospital and theatre costs; benefits, waiting periods and gaps depend on the policy and fund.
   140	- A cost guide breaks down components and funding options including private health insurance and early-access superannuation for eligible cases.
   141	- Do not quote a single dollar figure.
   142	
   143	---
   144	
   145	### Conversion CTA headline
   146	Direction: Empathetic and action-oriented without pressure. Acknowledge the reader has been carrying this for a long time, and frame the next step as a conversation about fit — not a commitment. Something in the spirit of: "You have carried this long enough. Let's talk about whether a gastric sleeve is the right next step for you."
   147	
   148	---
   149	
   150	### FAQs (8 questions)
   151	The page should answer all of the following. Writer to draft each answer in plain language, 3–5 sentences, compliant with AHPRA guidance.
   152	
   153	1. Is the gastric sleeve permanent, and can it be reversed?
   154	2. How much weight will I actually lose?
   155	3. Will I have to take vitamins for the rest of my life?
   156	4. How is a gastric sleeve different from a gastric bypass?
   157	5. What does recovery actually look like week to week?
   158	6. I get heartburn already — is the sleeve still right for me?
   159	7. Does Medicare cover gastric sleeve surgery?
   160	8. Do I need a GP referral to be seen?
   161	
   162	---
   163	
   164	## Tone and compliance notes
   165	- **Medical advertising:** No guarantees. Use phrases like "clinical averages", "results vary", "individual results depend on", "your surgeon will advise".
   166	- **AHPRA/TGA:** No testimonials, no before/after claims, no guaranteed outcomes, no exaggerated claims about effectiveness. GLP-1 / medical-weight-management advertising policy is pending a TGA/legal decision; use only active-ingredient or class names such as semaglutide, liraglutide and GLP-1, with no brand names or comparative claims until confirmed.
   167	- **Voice:** Plain-language, empathetic, direct. No hype. No "life-changing" or "transformative" without qualification. Write for a real person who is anxious and doing research — not for a brochure.
   168	- **Reader:** A Perth adult, likely 35–55, who has tried to lose weight many times before and is seriously but nervously considering surgery. They are sceptical of promises and respond to honesty.
   169	- **Surgeon credentials to include:** Dr Harsha Chandraratna (MBBS, FRACS, Bariatric & Upper GI Surgeon; personal bariatric surgery experience; senior lecturer Notre Dame University; operates at SJOG Murdoch and Subiaco) and Dr Stephen Watson (MBBS, FRACS, Bariatric Surgeon; accredited bariatric surgeon at SJOG Murdoch and Subiaco).
