   110	### Benefits (6 bullets)
   111	Each bullet should convey one clear, qualified benefit. Do not use superlatives.
   112	
   113	1. Weight loss — clinical averages of 60–70% of excess weight over 12–18 months are reported in some studies; individual results vary and no result is guaranteed.
   114	2. Reduced appetite — the procedure lowers ghrelin production, and some people may find smaller portions more satisfying; individual experiences vary.
   115	3. Improvement in weight-related conditions — type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, sleep apnoea, joint pain. Qualify that outcomes differ between individuals.
   116	4. No intestinal rerouting — food travels through the digestive tract in its normal order; the sleeve is generally less malabsorptive than a bypass, although vitamin and mineral monitoring is still needed.
   117	5. Keyhole approach — smaller incisions than open surgery; pain, hospital stay and recovery vary by individual and surgical circumstances.
   118	6. Single permanent procedure — no implanted device to adjust or maintain, unlike a gastric band.
