37:> Draft L228-L246: **How much does a gastric sleeve cost in Perth?**
47:> Draft L57-L61: Gastric sleeve surgery is highly effective for weight loss for two key reasons: first, because your stomach is smaller, you feel fuller, faster. Secondly, because a reduced stomach size minimises your body’s production of ghrelin (the ‘hunger hormone’), after gastric sleeve surgery, your appetite will also be reduced.
63:> Draft L307-L315: **7. Does Medicare cover gastric sleeve surgery?** Medicare item number 31575 attracts a rebate when eligibility criteria are met, offsetting part of the surgical fee. However, Medicare alone doesn't cover the full cost and hospital, theatre, and anaesthetist fees still apply. Private health insurance with a gold-tier (or equivalent) policy will cover the hospital component. A full fee breakdown is available to help you understand your out-of-pocket costs.
67:Why it matters: The cost-section replacement covers the earlier insurance claim at draft L236; this later FAQ repeats the absolute claim at L313. Coverage depends on fund rules, policy settings, waiting periods and gap arrangements, so the copy should not imply automatic cover.
73:> Draft L54-L55: It is typically performed ‘laparoscopically’ via keyhole surgery, which tends to mean an easier recovery than some more invasive procedures.
77:> Draft L173-L175: Significant, sustained weight loss; an estimated clinical average of 60–70% of excess weight over 12–18 months, though individual results vary.
81:> Draft L177-L179: Reduced appetite; the procedure reduces stomach size and lowers ghrelin (‘hunger hormone’) production, so smaller portions feel more satisfying.
85:> Draft L189-L191: Keyhole approach; means less scarring, generally less post-operative pain, and a shorter hospital stay (and recovery time) than open surgery.
89:> Draft L199-L199: Gastric sleeve surgery is well-established and generally safe, but it is still a major surgery which carries with it some real risks, all of which your surgeon will discuss with you in detail.
93:> Draft L201-L203: Staple line leak; though it is a serious potential complication and may require further treatment, it only occurs in ~1–3% of cases.
97:> Draft L205-L207: Reflux or heartburn can occur or (if pre-existing) worsen after a sleeve. In most people it settles, though for others it can be persistent.
101:> Draft L214-L217: Hair thinning in the months after surgery is typically linked to rapid weight loss and reduced nutritional intake; it’s usually temporary and improves as your intake stabilises.
105:> Draft L283-L289: **4. How is a gastric sleeve different from a gastric bypass?** A gastric sleeve reduces stomach size but leaves the digestive pathway unchanged. A gastric bypass both reduces the stomach *and* reroutes the small intestine, which can cause some nutrient malabsorption. The bypass may produce slightly greater weight loss and tends to suit patients with significant reflux better.
109:> Draft L300-L305: **6. I get heartburn already. Is a sleeve still right for me?** Pre-existing reflux is an important factor to raise with your surgeon, as a sleeve can actually worsen symptoms in some patients. For those with significant GORD, a gastric bypass is often the more suitable option as it tends to improve reflux rather than aggravate it.
