78:### Eligibility section
79:- BMI summary to anchor the section: "You do not need a very high BMI to start a conversation. A BMI of 27 or above, particularly where a weight-related health condition is present, is a reason to request an individual medical assessment for non-surgical options. For the Medicare-listed surgical pathway, current MBS thresholds refer to BMI 40+ or BMI 35+ with a major co-morbidity. We will help you understand what needs to be assessed."
81:  - BMI around 27+ with a condition such as type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, or sleep apnoea: invite an individual medical assessment for non-surgical options
82:  - Current MBS surgical thresholds: BMI 40+ or BMI 35+ with a major co-morbidity; clinical suitability is assessed individually
83:  - Higher BMI with prior attempts at weight loss that did not hold: surgical and non-surgical options can be assessed together
86:  - If the reader is unsure what can be assessed: that uncertainty is exactly what an initial consultation is for — explain the options without promising eligibility
146:- MBS surgical threshold: state BMI 40+ or BMI 35+ with a major co-morbidity as assessment guidance; BMI below 35 does not meet that MBS threshold. Do not present a lower-BMI statement as surgical eligibility.
149:- Reader: a Perth adult at the beginning of their research, possibly BMI 27–35, may not know the word "bariatric", may feel shame or exhaustion — the page must normalise the conversation before it can sell it
